{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a9caa2af8873ea2d", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tAI-Based Sound Effect Generation: A Narrative\n2\tReview of Generative Models Across Input\n3\tModalities\n4\t\n5\tarXiv:2608.03742v1 [cs.SD] 4 Aug 2026\n6\t\n7\tSandy Abdo [0009−0006−5128−1625] , Bill Kapralos [0000−0003−0434−3847] , Priyamvada\n8\tTripathi [0009−0005−5070−7420] , KC Collins [0000−0003−0695−7228] , and Adam\n9\tDubrowski [0000−0002−2074−0933]\n10\t\n11\tAbstract Sound effects play a crucial role in conveying actions, events, and environmental cues across digital applications, often requiring a high degree of variation and\n12\tcontextual adaptability. Artificial intelligence (AI)-driven audio generative models are\n13\trapidly growing in popularity and have the potential to transform the way sound is\n14\tsynthesized and used across various applications. In response to this growing momentum, this chapter reviews and analyzes recent AI-based generative models for sound\n15\teffect synthesis, with a focus on how different input modalities (text, visual, audio, and\n16\tmultimodal) affect the quality, controllability, and contextual relevance of the generated audio. It examines 30 peer-reviewed articles sourced from Google Scholar, IEEE\n17\tXplore, and the ACM Digital Library, exploring the evolution of AI generative models\n18\tover the past five years. The results show that multiple models achieved state-of-the-art\n19\tperformance, producing high-fidelity, semantically aligned, and increasingly temporally coherent sound effects across tasks. However, despite these advances, the review\n20\tidentifies persistent challenges, including limitations in temporal synchronization for\n21\tcomplex multi-event scenarios, gaps between objective metrics and human perception,\n22\tand trade-offs between controllability and generative diversity. Overall, the chapter\n23\thighlights that AI-driven sound effect generation is progressing toward more adaptive,\n24\tscalable, and context-aware systems, offering significant implications for future sound\n25\tdesign workflows and interactive media applications.\n26\t\n27\tSandy Abdo\n28\tOntario Tech University, Oshawa, ON, Canada, e-mail: sandy.abdo@ontariotechu.net\n29\tBill Kapralos\n30\tOntario Tech University, Oshawa, ON, Canada, e-mail: Bill.Kapralos@ontariotechu.ca\n31\tPriyamvada Tripathi\n32\tDurham College, Oshawa, ON, Canada, e-mail: Priyamvada.Tripathi@durhamcollege.ca\n33\tKC Collins\n34\tCarleton University, Ottawa, ON, Canada e-mail: kccollins@cunet.carleton.ca\n35\tAdam Dubrowski\n36\tOntario Tech University, Oshawa, ON, Canada, e-mail: adam.dubrowski@ontariotechu.net\n37\t\n38\t1\n39\t\n40\t\f2\n41\t\n42\tAbdo et al.\n43\t\n44\tKeywords Artificial intelligence · generative model · sound effect\n45\t\n46\t1 Introduction\n47\tSound is fundamental to how humans perceive, interpret, and interact with digital systems, and recent advances in artificial intelligence (AI) are transforming how audio\n48\tis created, processed, and experienced. Rather than functioning as a static or purely\n49\treactive element, modern audio systems increasingly leverage AI to become adaptive,\n50\tgenerative, and context-aware. This enables sound, including dialogue, sound effects,\n51\tambient noise, and music, to dynamically respond in real-time to user input, environmental conditions, and system states [6].\n52\tAmong these, sound effects play a particularly critical role in conveying events, actions, and spatial cues, making them essential in applications such as video games, film,\n53\tvirtual simulations, and user interfaces where immediate auditory feedback enhances\n54\trealism and user engagement [6, 38].\n55\tDesigning dynamic and responsive sound effects, however, remains a complex and\n56\tresource-intensive task. Sound designers must work through extensive audio databases,\n57\textract specific sound clips, and meticulously mix and manipulate sounds to achieve\n58\tthe desired auditory experience [6]. However, digital applications including games and\n59\tfilms may require thousands of sound effects to create experiences that resemble the realworld, especially since human pattern recognition capabilities make repeated sounds,\n60\tsuch as footsteps, quickly noticeable and detrimental to immersion if not sufficiently\n61\tvaried [6]. This repetitive workflow, combined with the need for constant variation,\n62\tcan be tedious and limit creative exploration. Automating some of these tasks using\n63\tAI could streamline the process, allowing designers to focus on experimenting with\n64\talternative soundscapes and enhancing the overall audio experience [12].\n65\tAs AI workflows become more integrated into digital applications, sound effect\n66\tgeneration is increasingly shifting toward procedural and adaptive approaches [40]. This\n67\tmakes it more difficult for sound designers to anticipate which sounds will be needed\n68\tand under what conditions, as user interactions or system-driven events can produce\n69\tunexpected and novel combinations of actions. Rare or unanticipated interactions may\n70\toccur that were not considered during the design process, creating a demand for flexible\n71\tand responsive audio systems. AI could synthesize plausible interaction sounds (e.g.,\n72\taction, background, and ambient) based on learned models of real-world acoustics [40].\n73\tIn such systems, sound must adapt dynamically not only to interactions but also to\n74\tcontinuously changing conditions. Acoustic properties such as reverberation, occlusion,\n75\tand layering may need to update in real-time as scenes evolve [31]. Additionally,\n76\tcontextual parameters, such as user state, system variables, or desired emotional tone,\n77\tcan influence how sound effects are generated and modified. For example, a character’s\n78\tfootsteps could vary depending on factors like movement intensity, physical load, or\n79\tcondition, resulting in more nuanced and responsive auditory feedback. At the same\n80\ttime, procedural systems can generate vast numbers of variations in actions, objects, and\n81\tevents, making it impractical to design unique sound effects for every possibility [31].\n82\t\n83\t\fAI-Based Sound Effect Generation\n84\t\n85\t3\n86\t\n87\tAI offers a scalable solution by dynamically generating and adapting sounds to match\n88\tan effectively unlimited range of scenarios.\n89\tBeyond large-scale productions, many digital applications are developed by smaller\n90\tstudios, independent creators, and researchers who may lack the resources or expertise\n91\trequired to produce high-quality sound effects. AI-driven tools can help bridge this\n92\tgap by lowering the cost and technical barriers to audio production, enabling more\n93\taccessible creation of rich and context-aware sound [12]. This is particularly valuable\n94\tin specialized or underrepresented domains, where tailored sound design is needed\n95\tbut traditional resources are limited, allowing a broader range of creators to develop\n96\thigh-quality auditory experiences.\n97\tThe purpose of this chapter is to provide an overview of audio generative AI, and to\n98\tidentify the available tools and models in the current literature that can generate audio\n99\tfrom prompts such as text, video, images, or other audio. This review maps current\n100\tknowledge, identifies trends, and guides future work in audio generative AI.\n101\t\n102\t2 Research Strategy\n103\tThis chapter is a review that follows the structure of a narrative literature review, as\n104\toutlined by Oxman et al. [34]. A narrative review serves to examine the literature\n105\tcomprehensively, offering a broad summary of the field. This approach is especially\n106\tbeneficial for readers new to the subject, providing them with valuable insights [16].\n107\tWhen crafting this chapter, we focused primarily on journal and conference papers,\n108\trather than for instance patents that may describe proprietary tools. The search was\n109\tcarried out using the Google Scholar, IEEE Xplore, and the ACM Digital Library\n110\tdatabases between 6 February 2024, and 4 March 2025. We limited scope to these\n111\tpapers published within the past five years due to the rapidly changing nature of the\n112\tfield. Earlier models were included only for context and were not part of the search\n113\tpool. IEEE Xplore, the ACM Digital Library, and Google Scholar were selected as the\n114\tprimary databases for this chapter due to their comprehensive coverage of research in\n115\tcomputer science, engineering, and AI, fields most relevant to generative audio models.\n116\tIEEE Xplore and ACM are leading publishers of conference proceedings and journals\n117\tin AI and signal processing, providing access to cutting-edge research and original\n118\tstudies. Google Scholar complements these sources by offering broad, interdisciplinary\n119\tcoverage. Searches in other databases, such as Scopus and Web of Science, largely\n120\treturned results already indexed in IEEE or ACM databases, indicating significant\n121\toverlap and reinforcing the suitability of the chosen databases.\n122\tAdditionally, the review focused on original, peer-reviewed research articles appearing in refereed journals and conference proceedings, written in English and present\n123\tmodels capable of generating sound effects. Models that primarily generate speech\n124\tor music were excluded, as these areas have already been extensively studied and are\n125\tcovered by several recent review articles [4,7,20,27,32]. The research query was: (“Artificial intelligence” OR AI OR “language model” OR LLM OR “Deep Learning” OR\n126\t“neural network”) AND (“Text-to-Audio” OR “video-to-audio” OR “visual-to-audio”\n127\tOR “audio-to-audio” OR “image-to-audio”) AND (“sound effect” OR “audio effect”\n128\t\n129\t\f4\n130\t\n131\tAbdo et al.\n132\t\n133\tOR “game audio” OR “game sound”) AND (design* OR creat* OR generat* OR\n134\tdevelop*).\n135\t\n136\t2.1 Results\n137\tThe search initially yielded 204 articles. After removing duplicates and non–peerreviewed articles [39], 60 abstracts were screened to assess relevance, specifically\n138\twhether the study introduced an audio generative model. To ensure the relevance and\n139\tquality of the reviewed literature, predefined inclusion and exclusion criteria were\n140\tapplied during both abstract screening and full-text review stages. Eligible studies\n141\twere required to be peer-reviewed journal or conference papers published within the\n142\tlast five years, written in English, and presenting original research on artificial intelligence–based generative models capable of producing sound effects. Studies were\n143\texcluded if they were non–peer-reviewed (e.g., preprints, theses, patents, or technical\n144\treports), review articles, surveys, or editorials. Additionally, works focusing primarily\n145\ton speech or music generation, lacking a clear generative modeling component, or not\n146\tproducing audio as a primary output were omitted.\n147\tOut of the 60 abstracts screened, 43 articles met the inclusion criteria. Following\n148\ta detailed full-text examination, 30 of these were deemed relevant to the review, with\n149\tfurther exclusions applied to studies lacking sufficient methodological detail or proper\n150\tevaluation to ensure analytical rigor. Although this is a narrative review, the search and\n151\tscreening process was documented transparently, and a summary of the information\n152\tflow through the phases of the review is provided in the Preferred Reporting Items for\n153\tSystematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) flow diagram [17] shown in Figure\n154\t1.\n155\tThe remainder of this review is organized around four key themes identified in the\n156\tliterature: (i) text-to-audio models, which take text as input; (ii) visual-to-audio models,\n157\twhich take images or videos as input; (iii) audio-to-audio models, which use audio as\n158\tinput; and (iv) multimodal audio generative models, which incorporate multiple input\n159\tmodalities while producing audio as output. Each of these themes is discussed in a\n160\tdedicated subsection. Table 1 provides an overview of the models that will be discussed\n161\tin this chapter.\n162\tTable 1: Summary of audio-generative models.\n163\tModel\n164\t\n165\tArchitecture\n166\t\n167\tAudioLDM\n168\t\n169\tLDM + CLAP\n170\t\n171\tTango\n172\t\n173\tLDM + Flan-T5\n174\t+ VAE\n175\t\n176\tEvaluation\n177\tAttributes\n178\tMetrics\n179\tText-to-Audio\n180\tFD, IS, KL,\n181\tEfficient, scalable sound generation\n182\tOVL, REL\n183\twith high audio fidelity\n184\tFAD, KL, FD,\n185\tHigh audio quality with strong\n186\tOVL, REL\n187\tprompt relevance on small datasets\n188\tContinued on next page\n189\t\n190\t\fAI-Based Sound Effect Generation\n191\t\n192\tModel\n193\t\n194\tArchitecture\n195\t\n196\t5\n197\t\n198\tEvaluation\n199\tAttributes\n200\tMetrics\n201\tAudioLDM2\n202\tLOA + LDM +\n203\tCLAPScore,\n204\tDomain-agnostic model enabling\n205\tAudioMAE\n206\tFAD, KL, OVL,\n207\tsemantic audio representation\n208\tREL\n209\tSonifyAR\n210\tPbD+ LLM+\n211\tPreliminary\n212\tInteractive AR-based audio\n213\taudioLDM\n214\tusability\n215\tgeneration\n216\tevaluation\n217\tAuffusion\n218\tPixel VAE +\n219\tFD, FAD, KL,\n220\tT2I method adaptation for TTA\n221\tLDM\n222\tIS, CLAPScore,\n223\tgeneration\n224\tOVL, REL\n225\tTango 2\n226\tDiffusion-DPO FAD, KL, IS,\n227\tTemporal precision and flexible\n228\t+ CLAP\n229\tCLAPScore, audio generation across applications\n230\tOVL, REL\n231\tSRC-gAudio U-Net + LDM +\n232\tFD, KL, IS,\n233\tMulti-sampling rate training for\n234\tVAE +\n235\tFAD,\n236\timproved audio quality\n237\tHiFi-GAN\n238\tCLAPScore,\n239\tOGL, REL, AQ\n240\tRe-AudioLDM\n241\tRetrievalIS, FAD, KL, Retrieval-guided generation aligned\n242\tAugmented\n243\tCLAPScore\n244\twith real-world sounds\n245\tLDM\n246\tStable Audio\n247\tDiT +MLPs FDopenl3 score, Open-weight model for scalable\n248\tOpen\n249\tCLAPScore\n250\taudio generation\n251\tPicoAudio\n252\tU-Net + LDM MOS, FAD, F1, Fine-grained temporal controllability\n253\t𝑓 𝑟 𝑒𝑞\n254\t𝐿1\n255\tAudioComposer\n256\tLDM +\n257\tF1, ACC, MAE, Hierarchical semantic modeling for\n258\thierarchical\n259\tMOS\n260\timproved audio quality\n261\tdiffusion\n262\tVideo-to-Audio\n263\tV2RA-GAN\n264\tGAN\n265\tODG, OSG,\n266\tDirect waveform synthesis via\n267\tarchitectural\n268\tregression-based modeling\n269\tevaluation, user\n270\tstudy\n271\tFoleyGAN\n272\tBigGAN +\n273\tRetrieval\n274\tVisual-guided audio synthesis using\n275\tvisual action\n276\tAccuracy, IS,\n277\tspectrogram generation\n278\trecognition\n279\tFID, NDB, user\n280\tnetwork\n281\tstudy\n282\tFRIEREN\n283\tRFM + VAE +\n284\tFD, IS, KL,\n285\tTemporally aligned audio via\n286\tBigVGAN\n287\tKID, FAD, Acc,\n288\tODE-based sampling\n289\tMIS\n290\tContinued on next page\n291\t\n292\t\f6\n293\t\n294\tAbdo et al.\n295\t\n296\tModel\n297\t\n298\tArchitecture\n299\t\n300\tEvaluation\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "d38295fa-d1d9-438f-92d3-e425bdae9b8c", "uuid": "f19c955b-cf92-4127-8f11-435b36c46de4", "timestamp": "2026-08-05T08:21:40.989Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper.txt", "content": "AI-Based Sound Effect Generation: A Narrative\nReview of Generative Models Across Input\nModalities\n\narXiv:2608.03742v1 [cs.SD] 4 Aug 2026\n\nSandy Abdo [0009−0006−5128−1625] , Bill Kapralos [0000−0003−0434−3847] , Priyamvada\nTripathi [0009−0005−5070−7420] , KC Collins [0000−0003−0695−7228] , and Adam\nDubrowski [0000−0002−2074−0933]\n\nAbstract Sound effects play a crucial role in conveying actions, events, and environmental cues across digital applications, often requiring a high degree of variation and\ncontextual adaptability. Artificial intelligence (AI)-driven audio generative models are\nrapidly growing in popularity and have the potential to transform the way sound is\nsynthesized and used across various applications. In response to this growing momentum, this chapter reviews and analyzes recent AI-based generative models for sound\neffect synthesis, with a focus on how different input modalities (text, visual, audio, and\nmultimodal) affect the quality, controllability, and contextual relevance of the generated audio. It examines 30 peer-reviewed articles sourced from Google Scholar, IEEE\nXplore, and the ACM Digital Library, exploring the evolution of AI generative models\nover the past five years. The results show that multiple models achieved state-of-the-art\nperformance, producing high-fidelity, semantically aligned, and increasingly temporally coherent sound effects across tasks. However, despite these advances, the review\nidentifies persistent challenges, including limitations in temporal synchronization for\ncomplex multi-event scenarios, gaps between objective metrics and human perception,\nand trade-offs between controllability and generative diversity. Overall, the chapter\nhighlights that AI-driven sound effect generation is progressing toward more adaptive,\nscalable, and context-aware systems, offering significant implications for future sound\ndesign workflows and interactive media applications.\n\nSandy Abdo\nOntario Tech University, Oshawa, ON, Canada, e-mail: sandy.abdo@ontariotechu.net\nBill Kapralos\nOntario Tech University, Oshawa, ON, Canada, e-mail: Bill.Kapralos@ontariotechu.ca\nPriyamvada Tripathi\nDurham College, Oshawa, ON, Canada, e-mail: Priyamvada.Tripathi@durhamcollege.ca\nKC Collins\nCarleton University, Ottawa, ON, Canada e-mail: kccollins@cunet.carleton.ca\nAdam Dubrowski\nOntario Tech University, Oshawa, ON, Canada, e-mail: adam.dubrowski@ontariotechu.net\n\n1\n\n\f2\n\nAbdo et al.\n\nKeywords Artificial intelligence · generative model · sound effect\n\n1 Introduction\nSound is fundamental to how humans perceive, interpret, and interact with digital systems, and recent advances in artificial intelligence (AI) are transforming how audio\nis created, processed, and experienced. Rather than functioning as a static or purely\nreactive element, modern audio systems increasingly leverage AI to become adaptive,\ngenerative, and context-aware. This enables sound, including dialogue, sound effects,\nambient noise, and music, to dynamically respond in real-time to user input, environmental conditions, and system states [6].\nAmong these, sound effects play a particularly critical role in conveying events, actions, and spatial cues, making them essential in applications such as video games, film,\nvirtual simulations, and user interfaces where immediate auditory feedback enhances\nrealism and user engagement [6, 38].\nDesigning dynamic and responsive sound effects, however, remains a complex and\nresource-intensive task. Sound designers must work through extensive audio databases,\nextract specific sound clips, and meticulously mix and manipulate sounds to achieve\nthe desired auditory experience [6]. However, digital applications including games and\nfilms may require thousands of sound effects to create experiences that resemble the realworld, especially since human pattern recognition capabilities make repeated sounds,\nsuch as footsteps, quickly noticeable and detrimental to immersion if not sufficiently\nvaried [6]. This repetitive workflow, combined with the need for constant variation,\ncan be tedious and limit creative exploration. Automating some of these tasks using\nAI could streamline the process, allowing designers to focus on experimenting with\nalternative soundscapes and enhancing the overall audio experience [12].\nAs AI workflows become more integrated into digital applications, sound effect\ngeneration is increasingly shifting toward procedural and adaptive approaches [40]. This\nmakes it more difficult for sound designers to anticipate which sounds will be needed\nand under what conditions, as user interactions or system-driven events can produce\nunexpected and novel combinations of actions. Rare or unanticipated interactions may\noccur that were not considered during the design process, creating a demand for flexible\nand responsive audio systems. AI could synthesize plausible interaction sounds (e.g.,\naction, background, and ambient) based on learned models of real-world acoustics [40].\nIn such systems, sound must adapt dynamically not only to interactions but also to\ncontinuously changing conditions. Acoustic properties such as reverberation, occlusion,\nand layering may need to update in real-time as scenes evolve [31]. Additionally,\ncontextual parameters, such as user state, system variables, or desired emotional tone,\ncan influence how sound effects are generated and modified. For example, a character’s\nfootsteps could vary depending on factors like movement intensity, physical load, or\ncondition, resulting in more nuanced and responsive auditory feedback. At the same\ntime, procedural systems can generate vast numbers of variations in actions, objects, and\nevents, making it impractical to design unique sound effects for every possibility [31].\n\n\fAI-Based Sound Effect Generation\n\n3\n\nAI offers a scalable solution by dynamically generating and adapting sounds to match\nan effectively unlimited range of scenarios.\nBeyond large-scale productions, many digital applications are developed by smaller\nstudios, independent creators, and researchers who may lack the resources or expertise\nrequired to produce high-quality sound effects. AI-driven tools can help bridge this\ngap by lowering the cost and technical barriers to audio production, enabling more\naccessible creation of rich and context-aware sound [12]. This is particularly valuable\nin specialized or underrepresented domains, where tailored sound design is needed\nbut traditional resources are limited, allowing a broader range of creators to develop\nhigh-quality auditory experiences.\nThe purpose of this chapter is to provide an overview of audio generative AI, and to\nidentify the available tools and models in the current literature that can generate audio\nfrom prompts such as text, video, images, or other audio. This review maps current\nknowledge, identifies trends, and guides future work in audio generative AI.\n\n2 Research Strategy\nThis chapter is a review that follows the structure of a narrative literature review, as\noutlined by Oxman et al. [34]. A narrative review serves to examine the literature\ncomprehensively, offering a broad summary of the field. This approach is especially\nbeneficial for readers new to the subject, providing them with valuable insights [16].\nWhen crafting this chapter, we focused primarily on journal and conference papers,\nrather than for instance patents that may describe proprietary tools. The search was\ncarried out using the Google Scholar, IEEE Xplore, and the ACM Digital Library\ndatabases between 6 February 2024, and 4 March 2025. We limited scope to these\npapers published within the past five years due to the rapidly changing nature of the\nfield. Earlier models were included only for context and were not part of the search\npool. IEEE Xplore, the ACM Digital Library, and Google Scholar were selected as the\nprimary databases for this chapter due to their comprehensive coverage of research in\ncomputer science, engineering, and AI, fields most relevant to generative audio models.\nIEEE Xplore and ACM are leading publishers of conference proceedings and journals\nin AI and signal processing, providing access to cutting-edge research and original\nstudies. Google Scholar complements these sources by offering broad, interdisciplinary\ncoverage. Searches in other databases, such as Scopus and Web of Science, largely\nreturned results already indexed in IEEE or ACM databases, indicating significant\noverlap and reinforcing the suitability of the chosen databases.\nAdditionally, the review focused on original, peer-reviewed research articles appearing in refereed journals and conference proceedings, written in English and present\nmodels capable of generating sound effects. Models that primarily generate speech\nor music were excluded, as these areas have already been extensively studied and are\ncovered by several recent review articles [4,7,20,27,32]. The research query was: (“Artificial intelligence” OR AI OR “language model” OR LLM OR “Deep Learning” OR\n“neural network”) AND (“Text-to-Audio” OR “video-to-audio” OR “visual-to-audio”\nOR “audio-to-audio” OR “image-to-audio”) AND (“sound effect” OR “audio effect”\n\n\f4\n\nAbdo et al.\n\nOR “game audio” OR “game sound”) AND (design* OR creat* OR generat* OR\ndevelop*).\n\n2.1 Results\nThe search initially yielded 204 articles. After removing duplicates and non–peerreviewed articles [39], 60 abstracts were screened to assess relevance, specifically\nwhether the study introduced an audio generative model. To ensure the relevance and\nquality of the reviewed literature, predefined inclusion and exclusion criteria were\napplied during both abstract screening and full-text review stages. Eligible studies\nwere required to be peer-reviewed journal or conference papers published within the\nlast five years, written in English, and presenting original research on artificial intelligence–based generative models capable of producing sound effects. Studies were\nexcluded if they were non–peer-reviewed (e.g., preprints, theses, patents, or technical\nreports), review articles, surveys, or editorials. Additionally, works focusing primarily\non speech or music generation, lacking a clear generative modeling component, or not\nproducing audio as a primary output were omitted.\nOut of the 60 abstracts screened, 43 articles met the inclusion criteria. Following\na detailed full-text examination, 30 of these were deemed relevant to the review, with\nfurther exclusions applied to studies lacking sufficient methodological detail or proper\nevaluation to ensure analytical rigor. Although this is a narrative review, the search and\nscreening process was documented transparently, and a summary of the information\nflow through the phases of the review is provided in the Preferred Reporting Items for\nSystematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) flow diagram [17] shown in Figure\n1.\nThe remainder of this review is organized around four key themes identified in the\nliterature: (i) text-to-audio models, which take text as input; (ii) visual-to-audio models,\nwhich take images or videos as input; (iii) audio-to-audio models, which use audio as\ninput; and (iv) multimodal audio generative models, which incorporate multiple input\nmodalities while producing audio as output. Each of these themes is discussed in a\ndedicated subsection. Table 1 provides an overview of the models that will be discussed\nin this chapter.\nTable 1: Summary of audio-generative models.\nModel\n\nArchitecture\n\nAudioLDM\n\nLDM + CLAP\n\nTango\n\nLDM + Flan-T5\n+ VAE\n\nEvaluation\nAttributes\nMetrics\nText-to-Audio\nFD, IS, KL,\nEfficient, scalable sound generation\nOVL, REL\nwith high audio fidelity\nFAD, KL, FD,\nHigh audio quality with strong\nOVL, REL\nprompt relevance on small datasets\nContinued on next page\n\n\fAI-Based Sound Effect Generation\n\nModel\n\nArchitecture\n\n5\n\nEvaluation\nAttributes\nMetrics\nAudioLDM2\nLOA + LDM +\nCLAPScore,\nDomain-agnostic model enabling\nAudioMAE\nFAD, KL, OVL,\nsemantic audio representation\nREL\nSonifyAR\nPbD+ LLM+\nPreliminary\nInteractive AR-based audio\naudioLDM\nusability\ngeneration\nevaluation\nAuffusion\nPixel VAE +\nFD, FAD, KL,\nT2I method adaptation for TTA\nLDM\nIS, CLAPScore,\ngeneration\nOVL, REL\nTango 2\nDiffusion-DPO FAD, KL, IS,\nTemporal precision and flexible\n+ CLAP\nCLAPScore, audio generation across applications\nOVL, REL\nSRC-gAudio U-Net + LDM +\nFD, KL, IS,\nMulti-sampling rate training for\nVAE +\nFAD,\nimproved audio quality\nHiFi-GAN\nCLAPScore,\nOGL, REL, AQ\nRe-AudioLDM\nRetrievalIS, FAD, KL, Retrieval-guided generation aligned\nAugmented\nCLAPScore\nwith real-world sounds\nLDM\nStable Audio\nDiT +MLPs FDopenl3 score, Open-weight model for scalable\nOpen\nCLAPScore\naudio generation\nPicoAudio\nU-Net + LDM MOS, FAD, F1, Fine-grained temporal controllability\n𝑓 𝑟 𝑒𝑞\n𝐿1\nAudioComposer\nLDM +\nF1, ACC, MAE, Hierarchical semantic modeling for\nhierarchical\nMOS\nimproved audio quality\ndiffusion\nVideo-to-Audio\nV2RA-GAN\nGAN\nODG, OSG,\nDirect waveform synthesis via\narchitectural\nregression-based modeling\nevaluation, user\nstudy\nFoleyGAN\nBigGAN +\nRetrieval\nVisual-guided audio synthesis using\nvisual action\nAccuracy, IS,\nspectrogram generation\nrecognition\nFID, NDB, user\nnetwork\nstudy\nFRIEREN\nRFM + VAE +\nFD, IS, KL,\nTemporally aligned audio via\nBigVGAN\nKID, FAD, Acc,\nODE-based sampling\nMIS\nContinued on next page\n\n\f6\n\nAbdo et al.\n\nModel\n\nArchitecture\n\nEvaluation", "numLines": 300, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 1419}}}
