{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-ad0e3ab2228f1a26", "type": "tool_result", "content": "=== 2604.22209 ===\n# UniSonate: A Unified Model for Speech, Music, and Sound Effect Generation with Text Instructions\narXiv: 2604.22209\nAuthors: \nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We propose UniSonate, the first flow-matching framework to unify TTS, TTM, and TTA tasks under a consistent, reference-free natural language instruction interface.\n  2. We introduce Dynamic Token Injection, a mechanism that symbolically represents unstructured acoustic events, enabling precise duration control for sound effects within a phoneme-driven architecture.\n  3. Extensive experiments demonstrate that UniSonate achieves state-of-the-art performance in instruction-based TTS and TTM while exhibiting positive transfer where joint training enhances structural coherence and prosodic expressiveness over single-task baselines.\n\nTags: TTS, TTS/架构-NAR-Flow, TTS/控制-NL描述, TTS/功能-多任务统一\n=== 2605.27838 ===\n# Dasheng AudioGen: A Unified Model for Generating Coherent Audio Scenes from Text\narXiv: 2605.27838\nAuthors: \nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: \nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We propose Dasheng AudioGen, a unified text-to-audio framework that jointly generates intelligible speech, music, sound effects, and environmental acoustics within a single audio scene without domain-specific modeling.\n  2. We introduce structured multi-view captions, a layered design that provides fine-grained supervision and disentangled control over different audio components while remaining compatible with agentic systems.\n  3. We introduce a unified semantic-acoustic representation based on DashengTokenizer as the shared latent space, which provides semantic priors for efficient training and sufficient capacity to disentangle and fuse concurrent audio components.\n  4. We establish a comprehensive evaluation pipeline for audio scene generation, demonstrating that Dasheng AudioGen substantially outperforms expert pipelines in mixed-audio scenes while remaining competitive with specialized models.\n\nTags: method, experiments, we propose, we introduce\n=== 2606.09098 ===\n# HoliDubber: Holistic Video Dubbing for Complex Acoustic Scenes via Text-Guided Audio Synthesis\narXiv: 2606.09098\nAuthors: Wenhao Guan, Yifan Duan, Junxi Liu, Yu Gu, Feng Dang, Kaidi Wang, Qingyang Hong, Lin Li, Xie Chen\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: \nStatus: unread\n=== 2606.03672 ===\n# Foley-Omni: A Unified Multimodal Generation Model from Task-Level Audio Synthesis to Complete Video Soundtrack Generation\narXiv: 2606.03672\nAuthors: Ye Tao, Lupeng Liu, Xuenan Xu, Jiasun Feng, Jiarui Wang, Ying Qin, Shuiyang Mao, Wei Liu, Shuai Wang\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: \nStatus: unread\n=== 2606.07397 ===\n# Audio-Oscar: A Multi-Agent System for Complex Audio Scene Generation, Orchestration, and Refinement\narXiv: 2606.07397\nAuthors: Yifan Duan, Qixiang Xu, Hengtao Wu, Zhanxun Liu, Wenhao Guan, Junxi Liu, Ziyang Ma, Kelu Xu, Xie Chen\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: \nStatus: unread\n=== 2602.17097 ===\n# AudioChat: Unified Audio Storytelling, Editing, and Understanding with Transfusion Forcing\narXiv: 2602.17097\nAuthors: William Chen, Prem Seetharaman, Rithesh Kumar, Oriol Nieto, Shinji Watanabe, Justin Salamon, Zeyu Jin\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: audio_understanding\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We develop AudioCopilot, a scalable pipeline for generating realistic audio scenes from scratch.\n  2. With the data generated from AudioCopilot, we develop AudioChat, the first unified model for generating, editing, and understanding audio stories with both speech and non-speech audio via structured reasoning.\n  3. We propose three novel evaluation metrics that overcome the weaknesses of standard audio generation metrics by directly measuring the task performance of audio generation and editing models.\n\nTags: LALM, LALM/对话型-交错标记, LALM/潜在表示\n=== 2512.20211 ===\n# Aliasing-Free Neural Audio Synthesis\narXiv: 2512.20211\nAuthors: Yicheng Gu, Junan Zhang, Chaoren Wang, Jerry Li, Zhizheng Wu, Lauri Juvela\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: codec_vae\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We apply oversampling and anti-derivative anti-aliasing to the activation function to obtain its anti-aliased form, which eliminates \"folded-back\" aliasing artifacts while maintaining numerical stability and bounded gradients.\n  2. We replace the problematic ConvTranspose upsampling layer with a resampling approach combined with a noise-like high-pass filtered deterministic prior to avoid \"tonal artifacts\" and eliminate \"mirrored\" aliasing artifacts.\n  3. We introduce Pupu-Vocoder and Pupu-Codec, lightweight neural audio synthesis models built upon the proposed anti-aliased modules, and release high-quality pre-trained checkpoints to facilitate future research.\n  4. We construct a test signal benchmark to objectively demonstrate the effectiveness of the anti-aliased modules in suppressing aliasing artifacts.\n  5. We experimentally validate that the proposed models consistently outperform existing systems on singing voice, music, and audio synthesis while achieving comparable performance on speech.\n\nTags: Vocoder, 压缩\n=== 2303.13336 ===\n# A Survey on Audio Diffusion Models: Text To Speech Synthesis and Enhancement in Generative AI\narXiv: 2303.13336\nAuthors: Chenshuang Zhang, Chaoning Zhang, Sheng Zheng, Mengchun Zhang, Maryam Qamar, Sung-Ho Bae, In So Kweon\nYear: 2023 | Venue: \nTopic: \nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Provides the first comprehensive survey specifically focused on recent progress in diffusion-based speech synthesis.\n  2. Categorizes text-to-speech diffusion methods into three distinct groups based on the model stage: acoustic model, vocoder, and end-to-end framework.\n  3. Classifies speech enhancement tasks into two types depending on whether specific signals are removed from or added to the input speech.\n  4. Includes comparisons of experimental results and detailed discussions across the reviewed methods.\n\nTags: TTS/架构-NAR-Diffusion, Vocoder/Diffusion", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "d38295fa-d1d9-438f-92d3-e425bdae9b8c", "uuid": "fba234db-4d16-4a39-9c64-e0e93a53b3fa", "timestamp": "2026-08-05T08:32:39.461Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "=== 2604.22209 ===\n# UniSonate: A Unified Model for Speech, Music, and Sound Effect Generation with Text Instructions\narXiv: 2604.22209\nAuthors: \nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We propose UniSonate, the first flow-matching framework to unify TTS, TTM, and TTA tasks under a consistent, reference-free natural language instruction interface.\n  2. We introduce Dynamic Token Injection, a mechanism that symbolically represents unstructured acoustic events, enabling precise duration control for sound effects within a phoneme-driven architecture.\n  3. Extensive experiments demonstrate that UniSonate achieves state-of-the-art performance in instruction-based TTS and TTM while exhibiting positive transfer where joint training enhances structural coherence and prosodic expressiveness over single-task baselines.\n\nTags: TTS, TTS/架构-NAR-Flow, TTS/控制-NL描述, TTS/功能-多任务统一\n=== 2605.27838 ===\n# Dasheng AudioGen: A Unified Model for Generating Coherent Audio Scenes from Text\narXiv: 2605.27838\nAuthors: \nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: \nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We propose Dasheng AudioGen, a unified text-to-audio framework that jointly generates intelligible speech, music, sound effects, and environmental acoustics within a single audio scene without domain-specific modeling.\n  2. We introduce structured multi-view captions, a layered design that provides fine-grained supervision and disentangled control over different audio components while remaining compatible with agentic systems.\n  3